WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.180
Converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA is not one reaction but three, carried out in

2
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.140
sequence by a single complex.

3
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:19.260
First comes decarboxylation, where pyruvate loses carbon dioxide, and the remaining

4
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.340
two-carbon fragment is captured on thiamine pyrophosphate.

5
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.540
Second is oxidation, where two electrons leave and the fragment is handed to the

6
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:36.020
lipoamide arm. Third is transfer to coenzyme A, producing acetyl-CoA.

7
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.600
Follow the carbon across the three boxes and follow the electrons separately,

8
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.460
because those electrons end up reducing NAD⁺ to NADH.

9
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.520
Notice that each step uses its own coenzyme, and several of those coenzymes trace

10
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:53.700
back to B vitamins.

11
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:57.773
Watch the lipoamide arm make its rounds.

12
00:00:58.773 --> 00:01:04.113
Tethered to the E2 core, it first swings to E1 and picks up the acetyl group,

13
00:01:04.473 --> 00:01:06.053
becoming acetyl-lipoamide.

14
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:12.973
Next, it pivots back to E2 and delivers that acetyl unit to coenzyme A, releasing

15
00:01:12.973 --> 00:01:17.593
acetyl-CoA and leaving the arm reduced as dihydrolipoamide.

16
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:24.773
Finally, it reaches E3 to be reoxidized, regenerating the disulfide so the cycle can

17
00:01:24.773 --> 00:01:30.813
repeat. Because the arm rides on a mobile lipoyl domain and the E2 core has many

18
00:01:30.813 --> 00:01:36.733
copies, 24 or 60 depending on the organism, all three active sites stay within

19
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:39.453
reach. The intermediates never escape.

20
00:01:39.993 --> 00:01:42.653
They ride the tethered arm from station to station.

21
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:49.693
Let me put the whole mechanism on one timeline so you know exactly where NADH is

22
00:01:49.693 --> 00:01:57.673
born. Step one, decarboxylation on E1, releases carbon dioxide and makes no NADH.

23
00:01:58.073 --> 00:02:04.013
Step two, oxidative transfer, hands the hydroxyethyl group to lipoamide as it is

24
00:02:04.013 --> 00:02:07.633
oxidized to an acetyl group, still no NADH.

25
00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:14.953
Step three, acetyl transfer on E2, produces acetyl-CoA and leaves dihydrolipoamide,

26
00:02:15.453 --> 00:02:23.693
again, no NADH. Only in step four, regeneration on E3, is the reduced lipoamide reoxidized

27
00:02:23.693 --> 00:02:28.493
by FAD and the electrons flow onward to make NADH.

28
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:34.953
So three steps build the product and exactly one step, the last, generates the reducing

29
00:02:34.953 --> 00:02:35.953
power.

30
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:42.927
Enzyme E1 is the key regulatory site of the entire complex, and it is controlled by

31
00:02:42.927 --> 00:02:48.087
a simple on-off switch built from a kinase and a phosphatase that travel with the

32
00:02:48.087 --> 00:02:52.847
complex. A kinase phosphorylates E1 and inactivates it.

33
00:02:53.387 --> 00:02:56.927
A phosphatase removes that phosphate and reactivates it.

34
00:02:57.447 --> 00:02:59.947
The switch reads the cell's energy state.

35
00:02:59.947 --> 00:03:06.947
When ATP, acetyl-CoA and NADH are abundant, signaling plenty of fuel and product,

36
00:03:07.307 --> 00:03:08.767
the complex is inhibited.

37
00:03:09.627 --> 00:03:15.167
When ADP and pyruvate accumulate, signaling demand and available substrate, the

38
00:03:15.167 --> 00:03:16.327
complex is stimulated.

39
00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:22.427
So the gateway from glycolysis to the citric acid cycle opens only when the cell

40
00:03:22.427 --> 00:03:24.507
actually needs to burn pyruvate.
